=E2=80=9Crange anxiety.=E2=80=9D=0A=0AEV makers=E2=80=99 share prices ralli=
ed after the 2024 election, at which point =0ATesla=E2=80=99s Elon Musk was=
 about to become the president=E2=80=99s closest adviser. Since =0AMusk=E2=
=80=99s controversial pay package was cleared in court last December, its p=
rice =0Ahas dragged:=0A=0AHigher oil prices have helped China, which has le=
d the US in EV exports =0A<https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-09-=
11/china-sets-70-ev-goal-for-new-car-sales-in-five-year-plan=3Fcmpid=3DBBD1=
00526_AUT&utm_campaign=3Dauthers&utm_medium=3Demail&utm_source=3Dnewsletter=
&utm_term=3D261005&utm_content=3D9673>=0A since 2021. Beyond the billions o=
f yuan the country has poured into R&D, tax =0Arebates and credits, geopoli=
tical forces continue to shape the market. The =0Agreen transition, oil pri=
ce volatility since Russia=E2=80=99s invasion of Ukraine in =0A2022, the US=
 repeal of the EV tax credits and, now, war in Iran have all buoyed =0Adema=
nd.=0A=0AFor a Chinese economy still struggling to recover from the post-pa=
ndemic =0Ahousing market collapse, any catalyst for exports is welcome. As =
Gavekal =0AResearch=E2=80=99s A.J. Cortese points out, the current energy s=
hock played into the =0ACommunist Party=E2=80=99s goal to prioritize energy=
 security. Beyond being the world=E2=80=99s =0Aleading EV exporter, Chinese=
 companies are moving at breakneck pace to dominate =0Athe broader supply c=
hain behind these vehicles. This could further erode the =0Acompetitive adv=
antages the US still holds. Cortese says:=0AWe need to understand that the =
Chinese companies are no longer content with =0Asimply improving cost effic=
iencies for these components, but they=E2=80=99re trying to =0Adevelop more=
 integrated solutions that they can both deploy at home and =0Aincrease the=
ir margins elsewhere.=0AUS EV makers need a meaningful structural shift. Bl=
oombergNEF cut its outlook =0Afor the second consecutive year after the wit=
hdrawal of federal policy support. =0AEVs are now expected to account for o=
nly 17% of US passenger vehicle sales by =0A2030, down from estimates of 27=
% last year and 48% in 2024. Gavekal Research=E2=80=99s =0AArthur Kroeber a=
rgues the US shot itself in the foot:=0AThere=E2=80=99s probably a pathway =
for US companies to become very successful in the =0Along run in new energy=
 vehicles. But within a totally closed ecosystem, can you =0Ado that withou=
t very strong support from the federal government=3F=0AOverall vehicle affo=
rdability =0A<https://www.morningstar.com/news/marketwatch/20261003114/cars=
-have-become-unaffordable-for-many-americans-heres-what-the-numbers-show>=
=0A has declined as the Fed raises rates, while the threatened 50% tariffs =
on =0ACanadian autos would worsen the problem. But at least higher gas pric=
es have =0Agiven the EV sector a fighting chance again.=0A=0A=E2=80=94Richa=
rd Abbey=0A=0AGarbage In, Garbage Out: Data Edition=0A=0ASome things never =
change. The latest non-farm payrolls figures =0A<https://www.bloomberg.com/=
news/live-blog/2026-10-02/us-employment-report-for-september=3Fcmpid=3DBBD1=
00526_AUT&utm_campaign=3Dauthers&utm_medium=3Demail&utm_source=3Dnewsletter=
&utm_term=3D261005&utm_content=3D9673>=0A featured huge revisions and big s=
urprises, making clear judgments difficult. =0ADistinguishing signal and no=
ise is hard enough at the best of times, but the =0Aadded uncertainty fromh=
uge changes =0A<https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-10-03/world-ec=
onomy-latest-us-employers-dial-back-hiring-wage-growth-slows=3Fcmpid=3DBBD1=
00526_AUT&utm_campaign=3Dauthers&utm_medium=3Demail&utm_source=3Dnewsletter=
