published - payrolls have been revised downward by =0A60,000 jobs over the =
last two months - makes it almost impossible.=0A=0AThis raises the question=
s why markets are still geared to treat Non-Farm =0APayrolls Friday as such=
 an event. Here is a splenetic reaction to the latest =0Anumbers from Peter=
 Tchir of Academy Securities:=0AThousands and thousands of people staring a=
t screens. Not teenagers checking =0Aout TikTok, but highly educated, well-=
trained professionals, staring at =0Ascreens. Millions, even billions, mayb=
e even trillions of dollars, yen, euro, =0Aetc., poised to move around base=
d on the data that comes out at 8:30 a.m.Then =0Awe get another mishmash of=
 data, so different than expectations, with revisions =0Abigger than the nu=
mber itself, and are supposed to make something of the data=3F=0AHe conclud=
ed:=0AWe will use the numbers because =E2=80=9Cwe have nothing better=E2=80=
=9D but that increasing =0Aseems like a giant cop-out! Seriously, if you we=
re in a class and were told =0Abillions of dollars would be made or lost on=
 a number that even the people =0Alooking at the number don=E2=80=99t belie=
ve, you=E2=80=99d shake your head in incredulity, that =0Athis really was g=
oing on. Not just for a month or two, but for years and years!=0AI had a se=
nse of deja vu, and had a look through the archive. Almost exactly =0A20 ye=
ars ago, on Nov. 2, 2006, I published a similar quote from Nigel Bond, =0At=
hen a strategist at Barclays, formy old employers at the Financial Times =
=0A<https://www.ft.com/content/43f60ff4-6a98-11db-83d9-0000779e2340>:=0AThi=
s writer has always suspected that the bond market=E2=80=99s concentration =
on the =0Apayrolls number had very little to do with the economic import an=
d a good deal =0Ato do with an endemic gambling dependency. Rather like rou=
lette, the payrolls =0Anumber is more or less random, but within a reasonab=
ly well-defined range. The =0Aeconomics profession sets the range for the r=
oulette wheel and the =0Arandom-number generation techniques of the Bureau =
of Labor Statistics do the =0Arest.=0AHis conclusion then was that =E2=80=
=9Cbussing in grannies for a monthly Bingo-Friday =0Awould be the more opti=
mal way to allocate capital in the global bond market.=E2=80=9D=0A=0AWarsh =
does have a data task force working on what numbers the Fed should heed, =
=0Aand Tchir points to proliferating private sector attempts to measure emp=
loyment =0A- although many suffer from the legitimate noise that comes from=
 big =0Aweek-to-week and seasonal variations in hiring. A resolution is ove=
